# Write effective links

Put links in body text only. Keep them out of titles, card titles, summaries and headings.

## Choose which links to use

Do not write something twice. If a Civica page or the Home Assistant documentation already explains it, link there.

Link to a site outside Civica only when:

- it is the Home Assistant documentation
- the task can only be done on an integration’s or a manufacturer’s site, for example creating the account a cloud integration needs before it can connect
- people need information Civica does not hold, for example the safety instructions for a device
- a reliable site does the job better, for example with a device compatibility checker

Before you add an outside link, check that:

- it goes straight to the page people need, not the front page of the site
- people can read it for free, without signing up
- you can explain why you chose one shop or manufacturer over another that offers the same thing
- people can use it with a screen reader, a keyboard or a small phone screen
- you have read its privacy and cookie policies and found nothing that puts people at risk
- someone will check the link from time to time, because outside pages move, go stale or start redirecting somewhere unsuitable

When you add a link:

- offer the online route before a phone number or postal address, unless one of those works better for the people reading
- remove tracking and redirect parts from any address you paste in, for example from an email or a forum post
- for a Civica download, link to the page that offers it, not to the file
- for a document on another site, link to the page it sits on and name the document people need there

## Add links in appropriate places

Put each link inside the sentence where the reader needs it.

Do not:

- gather the links at the foot of the page
- add a loose list such as “Useful links” or “See also”
- use so many links that people cannot tell which one matters
- link to the same page again and again

## Write clear link text

Good link text:

- says what is at the other end, never “click here” or “more”
- starts with the words that matter
- is long enough to select easily: if it is one word, choose one of 3 syllables or more
- is different for each different page
- is the same, or means the same, each time it points to the same page
- says when the link leaves Civica or Home Assistant, for example “the [Zigbee Home Automation documentation](https://www.home-assistant.io/integrations/zha/) (opens Home Assistant website)”
- names the language if the page is not in English, for example “Zigbee adapter set-up guide (in German)”
- gives the format and size when you have to link to a file, for example “diagnostics file (JSON, 140KB)”

For a link to information, make the topic the link text, such as “Zigbee coordinators”. The destination page’s title is often the best choice.

For a link to a page where people carry out a task, open with the action, such as “Add a Zigbee device”.

<h3 id="wg-083">Describe the destination in the link text</h3>

A link takes people to a page. It never changes a device. On a dashboard, “Turn off heating” is a button, not a link.

<h3 id="wg-037">Say when a link leaves Home Assistant</h3>

Also say when a link starts a download or opens a new tab. Never put a password, a token or personal details in a link address.

## Be careful with anchor links

An anchor link takes people to one section of a page. That section can be:

- further up or down the same page
- on another page

Use them as little as you can. Landing part-way down a page can confuse people, especially some people with access needs.

Needing to jump within a page is a sign that the page is in the wrong order. Reorder it instead.

Linking to a section of another page is sometimes worth it, for example when people need only one section of a long integration page.

When you do this, make the link text say exactly what people will find, for example “read the interference section of the Zigbee Home Automation documentation to find out where to put your Zigbee adapter”. The link text tells people:

- where they are going: “the Zigbee Home Automation documentation”
- what to do there: “read the interference section”
- why: “to find out where to put your Zigbee adapter”

## Check services that collect personal data

Link only to forms and survey services that the Civica maintainers have approved.

If you are not sure, ask the maintainers before you publish, using the contact details in the [Civica privacy notice](../../community/privacy.html#how-to-contact-us).
